{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QcasVP3D4YZmQ1yW1UmzkJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013nwwmmYUkF8D8KQVeexPid","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic review. Your focus: **Method soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\"\n\nThe paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box, post-hoc framework that:\n1. Generates binary style counterfactuals along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs. casual)\n2. Verifies content preservation via NLI entailment (≥0.85 threshold) and BERTScore\n3. Measures causal style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison\n4. Compares pairwise vs. Likert measurement sensitivity\n\nKey results across 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash), 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), 3 axes (formality, tone, register):\n- C1: Significant style premiums detected (mean 66.3% win rate for formality, 7/9 cells p<0.05)\n- C2: Likert scoring fails to detect any bias (0/9 significant) while pairwise succeeds (7/9) — \"measurement gap\"\n- C3: Axis-dependent bias: formality (68.4%) > register (66.3%) > tone (63.4%); Gemini-Flash consistently strongest\n\nRobustness: n=500 scaled replication (p<10^-6), original-preference control (53.3%, p=0.362), rewriter ablation (Llama vs Qwen, Fisher p=0.42). Verbosity axis failed NLI verification (65%), presented as principled method boundary.\n\n## Paper Details (Tables)\n\nTable 1: Formality pairwise (LLMBar, n=100): GPT-4o 69.1% p=.006, Claude 64.0% p=.028, Gemini 72.4% p=.002\nTable 2: 3×3 matrix: 7/9 significant (Claude×MT-Bench borderline p=0.065)\nTable 3: Counterfactual quality: NLI pass 87-90%, BERTScore 0.835-0.851\nTable 4: Likert vs Pairwise: All 9 Likert p>0.14, 7/9 Pairwise p<0.05. Mean Likert p=0.37, Mean Pairwise p=0.021\nTable 5: Tone (LLMBar): GPT-4o 63.5% p=.038, Claude 58.3% p=.085†, Gemini 68.5% p=.006\nTable 6: Register (LLMBar): GPT-4o 66.0% p=.015, Claude 62.0% p=.042, Gemini 71.4% p=.003\nTable 7: Cross-axis: formality 68.4% 3/3 sig, register 66.3% 3/3, tone 63.4% 2/3, verbosity failed NLI\nTable 8: Original preference control: 53.3% win rate, p=0.362\nTable 9: Effect decomposition: CSD 69.1% p=.006 vs original pref 53.3% p=.362 → corrected ~65.8%\nTable 10: Scaled replication (Gemini×LLMBar): n=100 69.1% → n=500 68.1%, p<.001\nTable 11: Power analysis: n=100 power=0.72 for d=0.065, n=500 power=0.995\nTable 12: Rewriter ablation: Qwen 69.1% vs Llama 66.0%, Fisher p=0.42\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment plan lists 7 experiments: exp-001 through exp-009 (no exp-007). The paper covers:\n- exp-001 (multi-judge formality): ✓ covered in Table 1\n- exp-002 (multi-benchmark formality): ✓ covered in Table 2\n- exp-003 (Likert vs Pairwise): ✓ covered in Table 4\n- exp-004 (tone axis): ✓ covered in Table 5\n- exp-005 (original preference control): ✓ covered in Table 8\n- exp-006 (scaled replication n=500): ✓ covered in Table 10 (but only pilot vs full, not 5 subsamples)\n- exp-008 (register axis): ✓ covered in Table 6\n- exp-009 (non-Qwen rewriter control): ✓ covered in Table 12\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on these dimensions. For each, provide specific evidence and critique:\n\n1. **Method Validity**: Is the CSD pipeline (counterfactual generation → NLI/BERTScore verification → position-controlled pairwise → sign test) a sound causal inference approach? What are the assumptions and are they adequately tested?\n\n2. **Experimental Design**: Adequacy of n=100 per cell, choice of judges/benchmarks, statistical tests used (sign test, Wilson CI, Fisher exact). Any missing controls or confounds?\n\n3. **Content Verification**: Is NLI ≥0.85 + BERTScore sufficient to guarantee content preservation? Could systematic artifacts survive this filter?\n\n4. **The Measurement Gap Claim (C2)**: Is the Likert-Pairwise comparison fair? Could the gap be an artifact of different statistical tests (paired t-test vs sign test) or different effective sample sizes rather than a fundamental measurement difference?\n\n5. **Robustness Controls**: Are the original-preference control, scaled replication, and rewriter ablation adequate? What's missing?\n\n6. **Statistical Concerns**: Multiple comparisons correction? Selective reporting? Effect size interpretation?\n\n7. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to replicate? Missing information?\n\n8. **Coverage vs Plan**: The paper reports all 7 planned experiments but some in compressed form (e.g., scaled replication shows only pilot vs full, not the 5 subsamples). Is this adequate?\n\nProvide your review in structured format with:\n- Summary of contributions\n- Strengths (numbered)\n- Weaknesses (numbered, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2593,"cache_read_input_tokens":58630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2593},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69","uuid":"99ee5664-6a01-494b-9141-11395643e6df"}
