9, sarcasm injection -96% vs factual error -13% | Foundation: establishes style dominance |\n| LC-AlpacaEval | ICLR'25 | Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing | Foundation: single-axis causal, population-level |\n| RATE | ICML'25 | Double-rewrite ATE eliminates counterfactual systematic error | Direct competitor: same counterfactual paradigm |\n| Preference Leakage | ICLR'26 | Model family style leakage, contextual calibration debiasing | Competitor: different mechanism for style bias |\n| CASSE | 2026 | 12×12 style feature interaction matrix, causal entanglement | Competitor: multi-feature but prompt-engineering based |\n| Counterfactual Rhetorical | ? | Persona counterfactuals + Bradley-Terry aggregation | Competitor: counterfactual + pairwise but persona-level |\n| Soumik 2026 | ? | CoT as universal debiasing, debiasing is model-dependent | Competitor: debiasing focus |\n| FairJudge 2026 | ? | Training-side debiasing (SFT→DPO→GRPO), 8B beats 72B/671B | Competitor: training-side approach |\n\n## Community Standards\n- Top ML venues expect: ≥3 diverse judges, multiple benchmarks, statistical rigor (CIs, significance tests, multiple comparison correction)\n- Trend: causal inference over correlation, multi-dimensional analysis over single-axis\n- Research gap: multi-dimensional style causal decomposition (no one does per-axis causal decomposition for judges)\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on these dimensions:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the CSD framework genuinely novel vs. incremental combination of existing techniques (counterfactual generation + NLI verification + pairwise comparison)?\n   - How does it differentiate from RATE (which also uses double-rewrite ATE)?\n   - How does it differentiate from CASSE (which also does multi-feature analysis)?\n   - Is the Likert-Pairwise measurement gap a genuinely new finding?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Are key competitors adequately cited and differentiated?\n   - Is RATE cited? (The paper mentions \"double-rewrite\" in the original preference control but doesn't seem to cite RATE explicitly)\n   - Is the Counterfactual Rhetorical work cited?\n   - Missing citations?\n\n3. **Positioning & Framing**:\n   - Is the paper's claim to be \"the first black-box, post-hoc, per-axis causal style bias detection\" justified?\n   - Are the four gaps claimed in the intro (monolithic style, correlational, population-level, no per-axis causal) real gaps?\n\n4. **Significance of Contributions**:\n   - C1 (method works): Is demonstrating that counterfactual + pairwise detects bias a sufficient contribution, or is this somewhat expected?\n   - C2 (measurement gap): How important is this finding for the community?\n   - C3 (axis dependency): Is the ordering (formality > register > tone) surprising or predictable?\n\n5. **Scope & Ambition**:\n   - 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, 3 axes — is this sufficient scope for a top venue?\n   - What's missing that reviewers would expect?\n\n6. **Writing Quality**:\n   - Is the paper well-structured and clearly written?\n   - Related work section: too short? Missing nuance?\n\nProvide your review in structured format with:\n- Summary of contributions\n- Strengths (numbered)\n- Weaknesses (numbered, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n","uuid":"b3dbf5f1-b68e-43a3-9418-562ed71694ba","session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69"}
