{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XQ1HcQRGeCvGjpRfqndMX4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Experiment Plan — causal_style_decomp_judge\n2\t# Each experiment maps to a Core Claim in paper_framing.md\n3\t# Priority: P0 = must-run for submission, P1 = important, P2 = nice-to-have\n4\t\n5\texperiments:\n6\t  # === C1 (Method) + C3 (Axis-dependent): Multi-judge generalization ===\n7\t  - id: exp-001\n8\t    claim: [1, 3]\n9\t    title: \"Multi-judge formality pairwise\"\n10\t    description: >\n11\t      Replicate formality pairwise experiment on Claude-3.5/4-Sonnet and Gemini-2.0-Flash.\n12\t      Same counterfactuals (already generated), different judge APIs.\n13\t      Dual-direction pairwise with position control.\n14\t    priority: P0\n15\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0  # API-only, no GPU needed\n16\t    api_budget: \"~$20-30 per judge (200 pairwise calls × 2 directions)\"\n17\t    deps: []\n18\t    registry_id: null\n19\t    status: planned\n20\t\n21\t  - id: exp-002\n22\t    claim: [1, 3]\n23\t    title: \"Multi-benchmark formality pairwise\"\n24\t    description: >\n25\t      Run CSD pipeline on AlpacaEval and MT-Bench/Arena-Hard subsets.\n26\t      New counterfactual generation needed (different source texts).\n27\t      GPT-4o + Claude + Gemini as judges.\n28\t    priority: P0\n29\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4  # vLLM Qwen3-32B for counterfactual gen\n30\t    api_budget: \"~$60-90 (3 judges × 2 benchmarks × 200 pairwise calls)\"\n31\t    deps: [exp-001]\n32\t    registry_id: null\n33\t    status: planned\n34\t\n35\t  # === C2 (Measurement Insight): Likert vs Pairwise gap ===\n36\t  - id: exp-003\n37\t    claim: [2]\n38\t    title: \"Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across judges\"\n39\t    description: >\n40\t      For each judge × benchmark combination, compute both Likert ATE and\n41\t      pairwise win rate on the same counterfactual data. Systematic comparison\n42\t      of measurement sensitivity. If Likert detects strong effects (e.g. verbosity\n43\t      on some judge), that constrains C2 to \"Likert fails on weak signals\" rather\n44\t      than \"Likert always fails.\"\n45\t    priority: P0\n46\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0  # piggybacks on exp-001/002 data\n47\t    api_budget: \"included in exp-001/002\"\n48\t    deps: [exp-001, exp-002]\n49\t    registry_id: null\n50\t    status: planned\n51\t\n52\t  # === C3 (Axis-dependent): New surface-level axis ===\n53\t  - id: exp-004\n54\t    claim: [1, 3]\n55\t    title: \"Tone axis (assertive/hedging) counterfactual + pairwise\"\n56\t    description: >\n57\t      Test tone axis as second working dimension. Tone (assertive vs hedging)\n58\t      changes confidence/stance without changing information content — similar\n59\t      to formality in preserving semantics. Full pipeline: counterfactual gen →\n60\t      content verification → pairwise scoring (dual-direction) → ATE analysis.\n61\t      Multi-judge (GPT-4o + Claude + Gemini).\n62\t      NOTE: Run NLI pilot (10-20 samples) before full run — hedging changes\n63\t      certainty markers (\"X is true\" → \"X might be true\") and NLI may be\n64\t      sensitive to this. 5 min GPU + $2 API to validate before committing 4h + $90.\n65\t    priority: P0\n66\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4  # vLLM Qwen3-32B for counterfactual gen\n67\t    api_budget: \"~$60-90\"\n68\t    deps: []\n69\t    registry_id: null\n70\t    status: planned\n71\t\n72\t  # === RC3 mitigation: Original preference control ===\n73\t  - id: exp-005\n74\t    claim: [1]\n75\t    title: \"Pairwise RATE control (original vs double-rewrite)\"\n76\t    description: >\n77\t      Pairwise comparison between original text and its RATE double-rewrite\n78\t      (same style). Quantifies original preference confound independent of\n79\t      style. If win rate ≈ 50%, original preference is minimal. If significant,\n80\t      provides correction factor for C1 estimates. D010 shows ~12.2% original\n81\t      preference — this is C1's biggest vulnerability (RC3).\n82\t    priority: P0  # upgraded from P1 per Reviewer: original preference is C1's命门\n83\t    gpu_budget_hours: 2  # reuse existing double-rewrites or generate new\n84\t    api_budget: \"~$15-20\"\n85\t    deps: []\n86\t    registry_id: null\n87\t    status: planned\n88\t\n89\t  # === RC5 mitigation: Sample size ===\n90\t  - id: exp-006\n91\t    claim: [1, 2, 3]\n92\t    title: \"Scaled replication (n=300-500)\"\n93\t    description: >\n94\t      Scale up primary formality experiment from n=100 to n=300-500.\n95\t      Power analysis to determine minimum detectable effect size.\n96\t      Run on best-performing judge × benchmark combination from exp-001/002.\n97\t    priority: P1\n98\t    gpu_budget_hours: 8  # larger counterfactual generation\n99\t    api_budget: \"~$40-60\"\n100\t    deps: [exp-001, exp-002]\n101\t    registry_id: null\n102\t    status: planned\n103\t\n104\t  # === C3 supplementary: Verbosity recovery + boundary analysis ===\n105\t  - id: exp-007b\n106\t    claim: [3]\n107\t    title: \"Verbosity direction-aware NLI verification + full pipeline\"\n108\t    description: >\n109\t      D012: Fix content_verification.py to use direction-aware NLI for verbosity.\n110\t      Expansion (concise→verbose): backward NLI only (cf→orig).\n111\t      Compression (verbose→concise): forward NLI only (orig→cf).\n112\t      If H1 passes (≥80%), run full pipeline (scoring + pairwise + ATE).\n113\t      Success recovers verbosity as second working axis → Main competitiveness.\n114\t    priority: P1  # already dispatched to Agent (D012)\n115\t    gpu_budget_hours: 1  # NLI model on cuda:1 + API for scoring\n116\t    api_budget: \"~$30-40\"\n117\t    deps: []\n118\t    registry_id: verbosity_32b\n119\t    status: in_progress  # Agent executing D012\n120\t\n121\t  - id: exp-007\n122\t    claim: [3]\n123\t    title: \"Verbosity method boundary analysis\"\n124\t    description: >\n125\t      Regardless of exp-007b outcome, document bidirectional NLI's structural\n126\t      incompatibility with information-changing axes. Concise→verbose 46% vs\n127\t      verbose→concise 87% with bidirectional min(). Direction-aware NLI as\n128\t      methodological contribution. If exp-007b fails, verbosity serves as\n129\t      negative control.\n130\t    priority: P1\n131\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0  # analysis of existing data\n132\t    deps: [exp-007b]\n133\t    registry_id: verbosity_32b\n134\t    status: planned\n135\t\n136\t  # === Additional axes ===\n137\t  - id: exp-008\n138\t    claim: [3]\n139\t    title: \"Register axis (academic/conversational)\"\n140\t    description: >\n141\t      Third surface-level axis test. Academic vs conversational register\n142\t      changes vocabulary and sentence structure without changing information.\n143\t      If both tone and register work → strong C3 (3 working + 1 failing axis).\n144\t    priority: P2\n145\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4\n146\t    api_budget: \"~$60-90\"\n147\t    deps: [exp-004]\n148\t    registry_id: null\n149\t    status: planned\n150\t\n151\t  # === Method robustness ===\n152\t  - id: exp-009\n153\t    claim: [1]\n154\t    title: \"Non-Qwen rewriter control\"\n155\t    description: >\n156\t      Use a non-Qwen model (e.g. Llama-3.1-70B or Mistral) as rewriter\n157\t      to rule out Qwen-family artifacts in counterfactual generation.\n158\t      Compare NLI pass rate and pairwise results with Qwen3-32B.\n159\t    priority: P2\n160\t    gpu_budget_hours: 8  # vLLM for alternative model\n161\t    deps: [exp-001]\n162\t    registry_id: null\n163\t    status: planned\n164\t\n165\t# MVP experiments (completed, linked for reference)\n166\tmvp_experiments:\n167\t  - registry_id: mvp_8b_ate\n168\t    claim: [1, 2]\n169\t    result: \"H2 FAIL (Likert ATE=0.120, d=0.117, p=0.246)\"\n170\t  - registry_id: mvp_32b_ate\n171\t    claim: [1, 2]\n172\t    result: \"H2 FAIL (Likert ATE=0.102, d=0.104, p=0.332)\"\n173\t  - registry_id: pairwise_32b\n174\t    claim: [1, 2]\n175\t    result: \"H2 CONDITIONAL PASS (69.1%, p=0.006)\"\n176\t  - registry_id: verbosity_32b\n177\t    claim: [3]\n178\t    result: \"H1 FAIL (NLI 65%, method boundary)\"\n179\t\n180\t# Statistical method requirements (Reviewer, distilled-research standard)\n181\tstatistical_methods:\n182\t  primary: \"Bootstrap 95% CIs (n≥2000 resamples)\"\n183\t  comparison: \"McNemar test for paired judge comparisons\"\n184\t  multiple_testing: \"Holm-Bonferroni correction across judges × benchmarks × axes\"\n185\t  note: \"Introduce in exp-001, apply consistently across all experiments\"\n186\t\n187\t# Resource summary\n188\tresource_summary:\n189\t  total_gpu_hours: 31  # P0: 10h (incl exp-005 upgrade), P1: 9h, P2: 12h\n190\t  total_api_budget: \"$270-400\"\n191\t  gpu_available: \"4× RTX PRO 6000 96GB (westb-29275), cuda:3 unusable\"\n192\t  timeline_estimate: \"P0 experiments: 3-5 days; P1: +2-3 days; P2: +3-5 days\"\n193\t  parallel_batch_1: \"exp-001 + exp-004 (pilot) + exp-005 — all can start simultaneously\"\n194\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013nwwmmYUkF8D8KQVeexPid","session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69","uuid":"1e6c1d13-d0d6-482e-8416-cad487b87797","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:50:14.996Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013bm1Rzc2u8oPLeWjumxPsv","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  last_updated: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T09:30:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  total_papers: 10\\\\ncategories:\\\\n  - style-bias-documentation\\\\n  - judge-debiasing\\\\n  - causal-text-methods\\\\n  - evaluation-benchmarks\\\\npapers:\\\\n  sos_bench_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Style Over Substance: Failure Modes of LLM Judges in Alignment Benchmarking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Murugadoss et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.15268\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: style-bias-documentation\\\\n    role: foundation\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Largest meta-benchmark documenting style-over-substance failure in LLM judges across 152K data points. Establishes the core problem CSD addresses.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2409.15268\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  preference_leakage_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Preference Leakage: A Contamination Problem in LLM-as-a-judge\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01534\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: style-bias-documentation\\\\n    role: foundation\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Shows judges rely on 'stylistic regularities—tone consistency, sentence cadence, punctuation density'. Establishes style bias propagation chain from judge → preference data → reward model → policy.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2502.01534\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  fairjudge_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"FairJudge: An Adaptive, Debiased, and Consistent LLM-as-a-Judge\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06625\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: judge-debiasing\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Most directly relevant concurrent work. Trains dedicated judge via SFT→DPO→GRPO but requires retraining (white-box). CSD is black-box post-hoc—key differentiator.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2602.06625\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  rate_icml_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"RATE: Causal Explainability of Reward Models with Imperfect Counterfactuals\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Shen et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00000\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: causal-text-methods\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Provides the double-rewrite protocol for unbiased ATE estimation under imperfect counterfactuals. CSD adopts this protocol for Stage 1 counterfactual generation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n\\\\n  dml_chernozhukov_2018:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Double/Debiased Machine Learning for Treatment and Structural Parameters\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Chernozhukov, V.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Chetverikov, D.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Demirer, M.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Duflo, E.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Hansen, C.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Newey, W.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Robins, J.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2018\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Econometrics Journal\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"10.1111/ectj.12097\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: causal-text-methods\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Provides the partially linear DML framework CSD uses in Stage 2. Doubly-robust property ensures consistency if either outcome model or propensity model is correctly specified.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  lc_alpacaeval_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Length-Controlled AlpacaEval: A Simple Way to Debias Automatic Evaluators\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Dubois et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: judge-debiasing\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Demonstrates effective post-hoc regression debiasing but only controls single dimension (length). CSD extends to multi-dimensional interventional causal identification—key baseline.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2404.04475\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  casse_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Concise Agent is Less Expert: Revealing Side Effects of Using Style Features on Conversational Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"CASSE authors\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10809\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: style-bias-documentation\\\\n    role: foundation\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Documents cross-feature stylistic interactions across 12 style features. Shows conciseness reduces perceived expertise—supports CSD's legitimacy-aware correction design (α parameter).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2601.10809\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  counterfactual_rhetorical_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Counterfactual LLM-based Framework for Measuring Rhetorical Style\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Authors\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19908\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: causal-text-methods\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Highly similar pipeline (LLM counterfactual rewrite + statistical aggregation) but only quantifies style effects without correction. CSD completes the quantify→correct loop.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2512.19908\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  soumik_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Judging the Judges: A Systematic Evaluation of Bias Mitigation Strategies in LLM-as-a-Judge Pipelines\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Soumik et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.23178\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: judge-debiasing\\\\n    role: direct-competitor\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Tests 9 debiasing strategies across 5 judges. Best strategy (combined budget) achieves +11.2pp. CSD should directly compare against this. Effect sizes 0.76-0.92 validate our premise.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"2604.23178\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\\n  veitch_uai_2020:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Adapting Text Embeddings for Causal Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Veitch, V.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Sridhar, D.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Blei, D.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2020\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"UAI 2020\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12741\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: causal-text-methods\\\\n    role: method-component\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Establishes functional intervention framework for binary text treatments satisfying positivity. Theoretical foundation for CSD's binary style axes being well-posed interventions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"1905.12741\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 5502, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xda4qWKtdiijqLiau6RN1k","session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69","uuid":"68382a07-d175-4627-af1f-1abf6f6a392c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:50:14.964Z"}
