{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"afce457ada73084cd","tool_use_id":"toolu_013nwwmmYUkF8D8KQVeexPid","description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":88993,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":186801},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"8f8e7801-2b5f-48da-81fb-396ba34b2c40","session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PV12MnvKV2fVaRkfXg6hDM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VJd2ce2gPiXS3Ak2FhFKzp","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T211832","overall_score":"3.5 - Borderline Conference","report":"# Review Report — R1 (Method Soundness & Experimental Rigor)\n\n## Paper: \"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\"\n\n**Reviewer Focus:** Method soundness and experimental rigor\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Contributions\n\nThe paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box, post-hoc framework for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges. CSD generates binary style counterfactuals, verifies content preservation via NLI/BERTScore, and measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison. The paper makes three claims: (C1) CSD detects significant causal style bias across multiple judges/benchmarks/axes; (C2) Likert scoring systematically fails to detect biases that pairwise comparison reveals; (C3) style bias is axis-dependent. Robustness controls include an original-preference experiment, scaled replication, and rewriter ablation.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-motivated research question with clear practical relevance.** The problem of style bias in LLM judges is timely and consequential. The paper correctly identifies the gap between correlational evidence (SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage) and causal identification, and between single-axis treatment (LC-AlpacaEval) and multi-axis decomposition.\n\n**S2. Clean experimental design with appropriate position control.** The dual-direction pairwise protocol that excludes inconsistent pairs is a sound approach for isolating style preference from position bias. The sign test on the consistent subset is the correct non-parametric test for this design. Wilson confidence intervals are preferred over Wald intervals for proportions, which is good practice.\n\n**S3. The measurement gap finding (C2) is genuinely novel and practically important.** The systematic pattern where 7/9 cells show Likert-insignificant but pairwise-significant results, with zero reverse-pattern cells, is a striking observation. If it holds, this has immediate implications for how the community audits judge bias.\n\n**S4. Principled method boundary via verbosity failure.** Presenting verbosity's NLI verification failure as a method boundary rather than hiding it is commendable. This demonstrates intellectual honesty and helps users understand when CSD is applicable.\n\n**S5. Adequate robustness controls for a conference paper.** The original-preference control (53.3%, p=0.362), rewriter ablation (Fisher p=0.42), and scaled replication (n=500 confirming n=100) form a reasonable robustness package.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1 (Major). The \"causal\" claim is overstated given the identification strategy.** The paper uses the word \"causal\" extensively, but the causal identification rests on a strong and inadequately tested assumption: that the counterfactual rewrite changes *only* the target style dimension and nothing else. This is the Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption (SUTVA) analog for text interventions. Several concerns:\n\n- (a) Formality, register, and tone are not orthogonal dimensions. Shifting formality (e.g., contractions to full forms, casual vocabulary to professional) inevitably shifts register and potentially tone. The paper acknowledges that the effect size ordering \"correlates with the breadth of linguistic change\" (Section 6), which implicitly concedes that these axes overlap. If formality rewrites also shift register, the \"formality premium\" is really a \"formality+register premium,\" undermining the per-axis decomposition claim.\n- (b) The NLI+BERTScore verification checks content preservation but does *not* check style orthogonality. No experiment measures whether a formality rewrite also changes tone or register scores.\n- (c) Without a cross-axis contamination analysis, C3's ordering (formality > register > tone) could simply reflect the degree of overlap between axes rather than independent axis-specific bias.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a cross-axis contamination measurement: for each axis's rewrites, measure the change on the other axes (e.g., does a formality rewrite also shift register classifiers?). Without this, downgrade \"causal per-axis decomposition\" to \"causal style intervention along correlated axes.\"\n\n**W2 (Major). The Likert-Pairwise comparison (C2) is not an apples-to-apples comparison and the \"measurement gap\" may be partially artifactual.** Several confounds make this comparison less clean than presented:\n\n- (a) **Different statistical tests with different properties.** Likert uses a paired t-test on continuous scores; pairwise uses a sign test on binary outcomes. These have fundamentally different sensitivity profiles. The sign test on consistent pairs is a within-subject paired comparison that eliminates between-item variance by design. The paired t-test on Likert scores retains residual between-item variance through score anchoring effects. The paper acknowledges this (Section 6) but frames it as a property of the *measurement method* rather than the *statistical test*, which conflates the two.\n- (b) **Different effective sample sizes.** The Likert test uses all n=100 pairs (or ~88 NLI-passing pairs). The pairwise test uses only the ~55 consistent pairs. But the pairwise approach gets *two* observations per pair (both directions), effectively doubling the information. The paper does not account for this.\n- (c) **The power analysis is circular.** The power analysis in Table 11 shows that Likert has power 0.72 at n=100 for d=0.065. But d=0.065 is the *observed* Likert effect size, not an independently estimated true effect. If the true effect on the Likert scale is d=0.065, then Likert *should* be underpowered at n=100 --- that is the correct behavior of the test, not a flaw in the measurement method. The real question is: what is the true effect on each scale? If pairwise is more sensitive, then the true pairwise effect is larger, but that does not mean Likert \"misses\" the bias --- it means the bias is genuinely smaller on the Likert scale.\n- (d) **A fairer comparison** would use the same statistical framework. For instance: bootstrap the Likert paired differences and compare the confidence interval width to the pairwise CI width, controlling for effective sample size. Or: use a Likert-based sign test (does formal score > casual score for each pair?) to isolate the measurement scale from the statistical test.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a Likert sign test (binary: did formal score higher than casual? Ignore ties, sign test on the rest). If this also fails to detect bias, the measurement gap claim is strengthened. If it succeeds, the gap is about the *test* (t-test vs sign test), not the *measurement scale* (Likert vs pairwise), and the paper's framing needs revision.\n\n**W3 (Major). All results beyond GPT-4o x LLMBar x formality are imagined/projected, not empirically obtained.** This is the most critical concern. From the project records (decisions.yaml, memory.md, ROADMAP.md), the *only* real experimental results are:\n\n- MVP: GPT-4o x LLMBar x formality (pairwise 69.1%, p=0.006)\n- MVP: Verbosity axis failed (NLI 65%)\n- MVP: Likert ATE non-significant (d=0.104, p=0.332)\n\nAll other results --- Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, tone axis, register axis, original-preference control, scaled replication, rewriter ablation --- come from the `imagined_experiments.yaml` file and are projected numbers, not empirical measurements. The paper presents them as if they are real results with specific p-values. This is the \"dream/best\" tier paper, so this is expected by the framework, but any reviewer evaluating method soundness must flag: **the actual empirical evidence base is a single judge x single benchmark x single axis cell with n=55 consistent pairs.** The impressive 3x3x3 matrix is entirely hypothetical.\n\nIf the actual experiments produce results that differ from projections (e.g., if Claude shows no significant formality bias, or if the original-preference control shows significant confounding), multiple claims would collapse.\n\n**W4 (Major). No multiple comparison correction despite 27+ hypothesis tests.** The paper runs sign tests on 9 judge x benchmark cells for formality, 3 for tone, 3 for register, plus robustness tests. With 15+ primary tests at alpha=0.05, the expected false positive count under the null is ~0.75. The experiment_plan.yaml correctly specifies \"Holm-Bonferroni correction across judges x benchmarks x axes\" in the statistical methods requirements, but the paper does not apply it. After Holm-Bonferroni correction across the 9 formality cells, borderline results like Claude x AlpacaEval (p=0.048) and Claude x MT-Bench (p=0.065) would lose significance, changing the count from 7/9 to perhaps 5/9 or 6/9 significant.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W5 (Minor). The \"corrected estimate\" in Table 9 (65.8%) lacks formal justification.** The paper subtracts original preference (53.3% - 50% = 3.3%) from the formality premium (69.1%) to get ~65.8%. This arithmetic assumes additive decomposition of biases on the win-rate scale, which is not justified. Win rates are not additive in general. A proper correction would require a formal model (e.g., logistic decomposition).\n\n**W6 (Minor). Content verification thresholds appear to have been tuned during development.** The decisions log shows the NLI threshold was lowered from 0.95 (NLI_THRESHOLD_FULL in code) to 0.90 (NLI_THRESHOLD_MVP), and BERTScore was dropped as a gate entirely (D003) after low pass rates. The paper presents the final 0.85 NLI threshold as if it were pre-specified. While the final threshold is defensible, the post-hoc threshold selection should be disclosed, and a sensitivity analysis across thresholds (0.80, 0.85, 0.90, 0.95) should be provided.\n\n**W7 (Minor). Insufficient detail on the pairwise prompt.** The pairwise evaluation prompt (visible in the code) asks judges to compare \"overall quality (helpfulness, accuracy, completeness, coherence)\" --- it does not mention style. This is intentional (to see if the judge spontaneously prefers the styled version), but the specific prompt wording could affect results. No prompt sensitivity analysis is provided.\n\n**W8 (Minor). The consistency rate (~56%) means nearly half the data is discarded.** Only ~55 of ~98 valid pairs are consistent across positions. The inconsistent 44% are discarded as \"position-driven.\" But inconsistency could also reflect genuine uncertainty about which version is better, noise in judge behavior, or near-ties. Discarding these introduces selection bias: the consistent subset may over-represent cases where the style difference is most salient, inflating the win rate estimate.\n\n**W9 (Minor). Tone and register axes tested only on LLMBar.** Unlike formality (tested on 3 benchmarks), tone and register are tested on a single benchmark. This limits the generalizability claim for C3. The cross-axis comparison (Table 7) is therefore partially confounded with benchmark.\n\n**W10 (Minor). The paper lacks qualitative examples.** No counterfactual pairs are shown. Readers cannot assess whether the rewrites are natural, whether the style dimension is cleanly isolated, or what kinds of content changes might survive NLI filtering. Even 2-3 examples would substantially improve transparency.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. Can you provide a Likert-based sign test (did formal outscore casual for each pair?) as a control? This would disentangle the measurement scale (Likert vs pairwise) from the statistical test (t-test vs sign test) in the C2 claim.\n\nQ2. What is the cross-axis contamination? When you rewrite for formality, how much do tone and register change? A simple classifier-based measurement would suffice.\n\nQ3. Why is the NLI threshold 0.85 in the paper but 0.90 in the code (NLI_THRESHOLD_MVP)? Were thresholds changed during development, and if so, how sensitive are results to threshold choice?\n\nQ4. Have you considered what happens to the win rate if inconsistent pairs are included (e.g., assigned 0.5 each) rather than discarded? This would provide a more conservative estimate.\n\nQ5. The original-preference control is run only on GPT-4o x LLMBar. Given the strong direction asymmetry (78.8% vs 54.5%) observed in the MVP, shouldn't this control be replicated across judges to ensure it generalizes?\n\nQ6. The scaled replication (Table 10) uses a different judge-benchmark cell (Gemini x LLMBar) than the pilot (GPT-4o x LLMBar). This is not a true replication but a new experiment. Can you clarify?\n\nQ7. What is the inter-annotator agreement if the same judge is queried multiple times on the same pair? This would establish a reliability ceiling for the pairwise method.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Dimension Assessments\n\n### 1. Method Validity\nThe CSD pipeline is a reasonable approach for detecting style bias, and the position-controlled dual-trial design is sound. However, the \"causal\" framing oversells the identification strength. The key untested assumption --- that rewrites change only the target axis --- is structurally difficult to satisfy for correlated style dimensions (W1). The method is best described as \"interventional style bias detection\" rather than \"causal style decomposition.\"\n\n### 2. Experimental Design\nThe n=100 per cell design is adequate for effects of the observed magnitude (power ~0.85 for 68% win rate). The choice of judges is reasonable (three major providers). Statistical tests (sign test, Wilson CI, Fisher exact) are appropriate. However, absence of multiple comparison correction is a significant gap (W4), and the Likert-Pairwise comparison conflates measurement scale with statistical test (W2).\n\n### 3. Content Verification\nNLI >= 0.85 plus BERTScore is a reasonable but not sufficient guarantee. NLI models can be fooled by paraphrases that shift emphasis, add/remove qualifications, or change specificity --- none of which register as entailment failures. Systematic artifacts could survive: e.g., if formal rewrites systematically add more precise vocabulary, this could be perceived as higher quality *legitimately*, not as style bias. The paper does not test for this.\n\n### 4. The Measurement Gap Claim (C2)\nThis is the paper's strongest potential contribution but also its most fragile. The comparison is confounded by the different statistical tests used (W2). Without a Likert sign test control, it is impossible to determine whether the gap comes from the measurement scale or the test statistic. The power analysis argument is circular (W2c). If the authors can show that a Likert sign test also fails, C2 becomes much stronger.\n\n### 5. Robustness Controls\nThe original-preference control is well-designed but limited to one cell. The rewriter ablation (Qwen vs Llama) is adequate. The scaled replication confirms stability but uses a different cell than the pilot (W6 in questions). Missing: cross-axis contamination control, prompt sensitivity analysis, threshold sensitivity analysis.\n\n### 6. Statistical Concerns\nMultiple comparison correction is absent despite being specified in the experiment plan (W4). No selective reporting is apparent (the verbosity failure is honestly reported). Effect sizes (Cohen's h ~0.38) are in the small-to-medium range, which is meaningful for this domain. However, win rates around 65-70% could partially reflect systematic rewrite artifacts rather than pure style bias.\n\n### 7. Reproducibility\nThe method description is clear enough to replicate in principle. The code is provided (pairwise_scoring.py, content_verification.py). Missing: exact prompts for counterfactual generation (only described verbally), specific model versions/temperatures for rewriter, seed information for sample selection, raw data or data generation scripts.\n\n### 8. Coverage vs Plan\nThe paper covers all 7 planned experiments, which is commendable. The scaled replication (exp-006) shows pilot vs full but includes 5 subsample win rates in Table 10, which is adequate. However, all experiments beyond the MVP cell are imagined rather than real (W3), so \"coverage\" is nominal rather than empirical.\n\n---\n\n## Preliminary Score\n\n**Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference / Borderline Findings)**\n\nJustification: The research question is important and timely. The method design (position-controlled pairwise comparison) is sound. The measurement gap finding (C2) is potentially the strongest contribution. However, three major issues prevent a clear accept: (1) the causal claim is overstated without cross-axis contamination analysis; (2) the Likert-Pairwise comparison conflates measurement scale with statistical test; (3) the empirical base is currently a single cell, with all other results projected. If the experiments are actually run and the C2 claim is strengthened with a Likert sign test control, this could rise to a 4.0. As submitted (with imagined data), the paper is a well-designed framework paper with insufficient empirical validation for the breadth of its claims.\n\nFor EMNLP 2026: If all experiments are real and the statistical issues (multiple testing, C2 fairness) are addressed, this is competitive for Findings and possibly Main. In current form, it needs another revision cycle.\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** --- I am confident in the methodological assessment. I have examined the code, decisions log, and experimental record in detail. The main uncertainty is how the paper would change if real experiments replaced the imagined ones.\n\n---\n\n## Actionable Summary for Authors\n\n1. **Must do:** Run all experiments for real. The 3x3x3 matrix is the paper's backbone.\n2. **Must do:** Apply Holm-Bonferroni correction. Report corrected and uncorrected p-values.\n3. **Must do:** Add a Likert sign test to disentangle measurement scale from statistical test in C2.\n4. **Should do:** Measure cross-axis contamination (does formality rewrite change tone/register?).\n5. **Should do:** Provide threshold sensitivity analysis for NLI gate.\n6. **Should do:** Include 2-3 qualitative counterfactual examples.\n7. **Should do:** Discuss what happens if inconsistent pairs are included rather than discarded.\n8. **Nice to have:** Replicate original-preference control on Claude and Gemini.\n9. **Nice to have:** Prompt sensitivity analysis for the pairwise evaluation prompt.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":753,"cache_read_input_tokens":87480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":753,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013nwwmmYUkF8D8KQVeexPid","session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69","uuid":"4bcd13ee-1bb6-481b-b1b2-c3afbbfd90fb"}
