{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xda4qWKtdiijqLiau6RN1k","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3.0 (Findings)** | Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper's strongest contribution is C2 (the Likert-Pairwise measurement gap, 0/9 vs 7/9 significant), which is a genuinely novel and actionable finding. The CSD framework itself is an incremental combination of existing components (counterfactual rewriting from RATE, NLI verification, pairwise comparison). C3 (axis dependency) is weakened by untested axis independence between formality and register.\n\n**Three blocking issues identified:**\n1. RATE (ICML 2025) is not cited despite being the methodological foundation for the double-rewrite protocol\n2. Counterfactual Rhetorical (arXiv 2512.19908) is not cited despite being the closest pipeline analog\n3. Formality/register axis overlap is not tested or discussed — these may measure the same latent bias\n\n**Key recommendations:** Reframe with C2 as headline contribution; expand related work from 4 paragraphs to ~1.5 pages; test axis independence; apply Holm-Bonferroni correction across 27 tests; test continuous Likert to determine if the gap is integer-scale-specific.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/best/review_report.md`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ad43e1ed08f3ee2e9 (use SendMessage with to: 'ad43e1ed08f3ee2e9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 62016\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 161876</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69","uuid":"679c087c-f0a3-450f-ac90-441ec44af637","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:52:45.734Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind academic review. Your focus: **Novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\"\n\nThe paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box, post-hoc framework for per-axis causal style bias detection in LLM judges. Pipeline: counterfactual generation → NLI+BERTScore content verification → position-controlled pairwise comparison → sign test.\n\nThree claims:\n- C1 (Method Validity): CSD detects significant causal style bias across 3 judges × 3 benchmarks × 3 axes\n- C2 (Measurement Insight): Likert scoring systematically fails to detect biases that pairwise comparison reveals (0/9 vs 7/9 significant)\n- C3 (Axis Dependency): Style bias is axis-dependent (formality 68.4% > register 66.3% > tone 63.4%), with Gemini-Flash consistently most susceptible\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper, Section 2)\nThe paper's related work covers four areas briefly:\n1. **LLM-as-Judge Bias**: Cites SOS-Bench (style R=0.999), Preference Leakage (surface-style cues 76-92%), CASSE (12×12 feature matrix with causal entanglement). Position bias mentioned.\n2. **Length Bias Correction**: LC-AlpacaEval (regression-based length correction). Notes it doesn't generalize to other axes.\n3. **Counterfactual Evaluation**: Mentions counterfactual data augmentation in NLI/sentiment/fairness. Adapts to judge evaluation.\n4. **Causal Inference in NLP**: General trend of interventional methods. CSD as practical instantiation.\n\n## Key Competitor/Foundation Papers (from domain knowledge)\n| Paper | Venue | Core Claim | Relation to CSD |\n|-------|-------|------------|-----------------|\n| SOS-Bench | ICLR'25 | Style-overall R=0.999, sarcasm injection -96% vs factual error -13% | Foundation: establishes style dominance |\n| LC-AlpacaEval | ICLR'25 | Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing | Foundation: single-axis causal, population-level |\n| RATE | ICML'25 | Double-rewrite ATE eliminates counterfactual systematic error | Direct competitor: same counterfactual paradigm |\n| Preference Leakage | ICLR'26 | Model family style leakage, contextual calibration debiasing | Competitor: different mechanism for style bias |\n| CASSE | 2026 | 12×12 style feature interaction matrix, causal entanglement | Competitor: multi-feature but prompt-engineering based |\n| Counterfactual Rhetorical | ? | Persona counterfactuals + Bradley-Terry aggregation | Competitor: counterfactual + pairwise but persona-level |\n| Soumik 2026 | ? | CoT as universal debiasing, debiasing is model-dependent | Competitor: debiasing focus |\n| FairJudge 2026 | ? | Training-side debiasing (SFT→DPO→GRPO), 8B beats 72B/671B | Competitor: training-side approach |\n\n## Community Standards\n- Top ML venues expect: ≥3 diverse judges, multiple benchmarks, statistical rigor (CIs, significance tests, multiple comparison correction)\n- Trend: causal inference over correlation, multi-dimensional analysis over single-axis\n- Research gap: multi-dimensional style causal decomposition (no one does per-axis causal decomposition for judges)\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on these dimensions:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the CSD framework genuinely novel vs. incremental combination of existing techniques (counterfactual generation + NLI verification + pairwise comparison)?\n   - How does it differentiate from RATE (which also uses double-rewrite ATE)?\n   - How does it differentiate from CASSE (which also does multi-feature analysis)?\n   - Is the Likert-Pairwise measurement gap a genuinely new finding?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Are key competitors adequately cited and differentiated?\n   - Is RATE cited? (The paper mentions \"double-rewrite\" in the original preference control but doesn't seem to cite RATE explicitly)\n   - Is the Counterfactual Rhetorical work cited?\n   - Missing citations?\n\n3. **Positioning & Framing**:\n   - Is the paper's claim to be \"the first black-box, post-hoc, per-axis causal style bias detection\" justified?\n   - Are the four gaps claimed in the intro (monolithic style, correlational, population-level, no per-axis causal) real gaps?\n\n4. **Significance of Contributions**:\n   - C1 (method works): Is demonstrating that counterfactual + pairwise detects bias a sufficient contribution, or is this somewhat expected?\n   - C2 (measurement gap): How important is this finding for the community?\n   - C3 (axis dependency): Is the ordering (formality > register > tone) surprising or predictable?\n\n5. **Scope & Ambition**:\n   - 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, 3 axes — is this sufficient scope for a top venue?\n   - What's missing that reviewers would expect?\n\n6. **Writing Quality**:\n   - Is the paper well-structured and clearly written?\n   - Related work section: too short? Missing nuance?\n\nProvide your review in structured format with:\n- Summary of contributions\n- Strengths (numbered)\n- Weaknesses (numbered, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"ad43e1ed08f3ee2e9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3.0 (Findings)** | Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper's strongest contribution is C2 (the Likert-Pairwise measurement gap, 0/9 vs 7/9 significant), which is a genuinely novel and actionable finding. The CSD framework itself is an incremental combination of existing components (counterfactual rewriting from RATE, NLI verification, pairwise comparison). C3 (axis dependency) is weakened by untested axis independence between formality and register.\n\n**Three blocking issues identified:**\n1. RATE (ICML 2025) is not cited despite being the methodological foundation for the double-rewrite protocol\n2. Counterfactual Rhetorical (arXiv 2512.19908) is not cited despite being the closest pipeline analog\n3. Formality/register axis overlap is not tested or discussed — these may measure the same latent bias\n\n**Key recommendations:** Reframe with C2 as headline contribution; expand related work from 4 paragraphs to ~1.5 pages; test axis independence; apply Holm-Bonferroni correction across 27 tests; test continuous Likert to determine if the gap is integer-scale-specific.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/best/review_report.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":161876,"totalTokens":62016,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3744,"cache_read_input_tokens":57904,"output_tokens":367,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3744},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":367,"cache_read_input_tokens":57904,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3744,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3744,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"afce457ada73084cd","tool_use_id":"toolu_013nwwmmYUkF8D8KQVeexPid","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Method + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":93790,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":201822},"uuid":"8aa86830-63a4-4add-9f5e-24bfea80a15a","session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013nwwmmYUkF8D8KQVeexPid","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The review has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference)** | **Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**5 Strengths identified:** Well-motivated research question; clean position-controlled experimental design; genuinely novel measurement gap finding (C2); intellectually honest verbosity boundary; adequate robustness controls for a conference paper.\n\n**4 Major Weaknesses:**\n\n1. **Causal claim overstated (W1):** Formality, register, and tone are correlated axes. No cross-axis contamination measurement is provided. The per-axis decomposition claim requires orthogonality that is not tested.\n\n2. **Likert-Pairwise comparison is confounded (W2):** The \"measurement gap\" conflates the measurement scale (Likert vs pairwise) with the statistical test (paired t-test vs sign test). A Likert sign test would disentangle these. The power analysis argument is circular.\n\n3. **All results beyond GPT-4o x LLMBar x formality are imagined (W3):** The empirical base is a single cell (n=55 consistent pairs). The entire 3x3x3 matrix comes from `imagined_experiments.yaml`.\n\n4. **No multiple comparison correction (W4):** Despite 15+ hypothesis tests and the experiment plan specifying Holm-Bonferroni, no correction is applied. Borderline results (p=0.048, p=0.065) would likely lose significance.\n\n**6 Minor Weaknesses** covering: unjustified additive decomposition in Table 9, post-hoc threshold tuning, missing prompt sensitivity analysis, high data discard rate (~44%), tone/register tested on only one benchmark, and no qualitative examples.\n\n**9 Actionable items** prioritized as must-do / should-do / nice-to-have for revision.\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: afce457ada73084cd (use SendMessage with to: 'afce457ada73084cd' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 93537\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 201825</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eca547ca-feee-44fc-bb85-df598acb1e69","uuid":"3f683f25-4046-4a5a-bb95-ef3486a6ffb5","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:52:54.945Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic review. Your focus: **Method soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\"\n\nThe paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box, post-hoc framework that:\n1. Generates binary style counterfactuals along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs. casual)\n2. Verifies content preservation via NLI entailment (≥0.85 threshold) and BERTScore\n3. Measures causal style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison\n4. Compares pairwise vs. Likert measurement sensitivity\n\nKey results across 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash), 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), 3 axes (formality, tone, register):\n- C1: Significant style premiums detected (mean 66.3% win rate for formality, 7/9 cells p<0.05)\n- C2: Likert scoring fails to detect any bias (0/9 significant) while pairwise succeeds (7/9) — \"measurement gap\"\n- C3: Axis-dependent bias: formality (68.4%) > register (66.3%) > tone (63.4%); Gemini-Flash consistently strongest\n\nRobustness: n=500 scaled replication (p<10^-6), original-preference control (53.3%, p=0.362), rewriter ablation (Llama vs Qwen, Fisher p=0.42). Verbosity axis failed NLI verification (65%), presented as principled method boundary.\n\n## Paper Details (Tables)\n\nTable 1: Formality pairwise (LLMBar, n=100): GPT-4o 69.1% p=.006, Claude 64.0% p=.028, Gemini 72.4% p=.002\nTable 2: 3×3 matrix: 7/9 significant (Claude×MT-Bench borderline p=0.065)\nTable 3: Counterfactual quality: NLI pass 87-90%, BERTScore 0.835-0.851\nTable 4: Likert vs Pairwise: All 9 Likert p>0.14, 7/9 Pairwise p<0.05. Mean Likert p=0.37, Mean Pairwise p=0.021\nTable 5: Tone (LLMBar): GPT-4o 63.5% p=.038, Claude 58.3% p=.085†, Gemini 68.5% p=.006\nTable 6: Register (LLMBar): GPT-4o 66.0% p=.015, Claude 62.0% p=.042, Gemini 71.4% p=.003\nTable 7: Cross-axis: formality 68.4% 3/3 sig, register 66.3% 3/3, tone 63.4% 2/3, verbosity failed NLI\nTable 8: Original preference control: 53.3% win rate, p=0.362\nTable 9: Effect decomposition: CSD 69.1% p=.006 vs original pref 53.3% p=.362 → corrected ~65.8%\nTable 10: Scaled replication (Gemini×LLMBar): n=100 69.1% → n=500 68.1%, p<.001\nTable 11: Power analysis: n=100 power=0.72 for d=0.065, n=500 power=0.995\nTable 12: Rewriter ablation: Qwen 69.1% vs Llama 66.0%, Fisher p=0.42\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment plan lists 7 experiments: exp-001 through exp-009 (no exp-007). The paper covers:\n- exp-001 (multi-judge formality): ✓ covered in Table 1\n- exp-002 (multi-benchmark formality): ✓ covered in Table 2\n- exp-003 (Likert vs Pairwise): ✓ covered in Table 4\n- exp-004 (tone axis): ✓ covered in Table 5\n- exp-005 (original preference control): ✓ covered in Table 8\n- exp-006 (scaled replication n=500): ✓ covered in Table 10 (but only pilot vs full, not 5 subsamples)\n- exp-008 (register axis): ✓ covered in Table 6\n- exp-009 (non-Qwen rewriter control): ✓ covered in Table 12\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on these dimensions. For each, provide specific evidence and critique:\n\n1. **Method Validity**: Is the CSD pipeline (counterfactual generation → NLI/BERTScore verification → position-controlled pairwise → sign test) a sound causal inference approach? What are the assumptions and are they adequately tested?\n\n2. **Experimental Design**: Adequacy of n=100 per cell, choice of judges/benchmarks, statistical tests used (sign test, Wilson CI, Fisher exact). Any missing controls or confounds?\n\n3. **Content Verification**: Is NLI ≥0.85 + BERTScore sufficient to guarantee content preservation? Could systematic artifacts survive this filter?\n\n4. **The Measurement Gap Claim (C2)**: Is the Likert-Pairwise comparison fair? Could the gap be an artifact of different statistical tests (paired t-test vs sign test) or different effective sample sizes rather than a fundamental measurement difference?\n\n5. **Robustness Controls**: Are the original-preference control, scaled replication, and rewriter ablation adequate? What's missing?\n\n6. **Statistical Concerns**: Multiple comparisons correction? Selective reporting? Effect size interpretation?\n\n7. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to replicate? Missing information?\n\n8. **Coverage vs Plan**: The paper reports all 7 planned experiments but some in compressed form (e.g., scaled replication shows only pilot vs full, not the 5 subsamples). Is this adequate?\n\nProvide your review in structured format with:\n- Summary of contributions\n- Strengths (numbered)\n- Weaknesses (numbered, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"afce457ada73084cd","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The review has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference)** | **Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**5 Strengths identified:** Well-motivated research question; clean position-controlled experimental design; genuinely novel measurement gap finding (C2); intellectually honest verbosity boundary; adequate robustness controls for a conference paper.\n\n**4 Major Weaknesses:**\n\n1. **Causal claim overstated (W1):** Formality, register, and tone are correlated axes. No cross-axis contamination measurement is provided. The per-axis decomposition claim requires orthogonality that is not tested.\n\n2. **Likert-Pairwise comparison is confounded (W2):** The \"measurement gap\" conflates the measurement scale (Likert vs pairwise) with the statistical test (paired t-test vs sign test). A Likert sign test would disentangle these. The power analysis argument is circular.\n\n3. **All results beyond GPT-4o x LLMBar x formality are imagined (W3):** The empirical base is a single cell (n=55 consistent pairs). The entire 3x3x3 matrix comes from `imagined_experiments.yaml`.\n\n4. **No multiple comparison correction (W4):** Despite 15+ hypothesis tests and the experiment plan specifying Holm-Bonferroni, no correction is applied. Borderline results (p=0.048, p=0.065) would likely lose significance.\n\n**6 Minor Weaknesses** covering: unjustified additive decomposition in Table 9, post-hoc threshold tuning, missing prompt sensitivity analysis, high data discard rate (~44%), tone/register tested on only one benchmark, and no qualitative examples.\n\n**9 Actionable items** prioritized as must-do / should-do / nice-to-have for revision.\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":201825,"totalTokens":93537,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4798,"cache_read_input_tokens":88233,"output_tokens":505,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4798},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":505,"cache_read_input_tokens":88233,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4798,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4798,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1